{"key":"att_bch_barbarian_civic_minor","name":"Town Communal Grounds","description":"Town communal grounds provide cultural income and increased stability through the presence of a chieftain's or warlord's building.","category":"Economy","campaign":"main","builders":["att_sub_cult_barbarian_celtic","att_sub_cult_barbarian_germanic_east","att_sub_cult_barbarian_germanic_north","att_sub_cult_barbarian_germanic_west"],"levels":[{"key":"att_bld_barbarian_civic_minor_chieftain_1_legendary","tier":1,"cost":1000,"turns":3,"upkeep":0,"capital_only":false,"religion":"","resource":"","techs":[],"upgrades":["att_bld_barbarian_civic_minor_chieftain_2_legendary"],"from":[],"variants":[{"id":"att_bld_barbarian_civic_minor_chieftain_1_legendaryatt_cult_barbarian","subcultures":["att_sub_cult_barbarian_celtic","att_sub_cult_barbarian_germanic_east","att_sub_cult_barbarian_germanic_north","att_sub_cult_barbarian_germanic_west"],"faction":"","name":"Chieftain's House","picture":"att_bld_barbarian_civic_major_1_large","short":"Everyone needs a place to gather to feel part of society.","flavour":"Roman and Greek historians often described the barbarian tribes as wandering people who lived for the hunt and knew little of farming and construction. However, nothing could be further from the truth; many remains have been discovered that show barbarians were far more civilised than previously thought. A number of structures have been discovered that show that so-called 'barbarians' had a firm grasp of building techniques and were able to construct solid homes and defences, many of which have survived for thousands of years."}],"effects":[{"text":"Wealth: 115 from culture","tone":"good"},{"text":"Public order: +3","tone":"good"},{"text":"Enables agent recruitment: Spies","tone":"good"}],"units":[],"garrison":[]},{"key":"att_bld_barbarian_civic_minor_chieftain_2_legendary","tier":2,"cost":1200,"turns":4,"upkeep":0,"capital_only":false,"religion":"","resource":"","techs":[],"upgrades":["att_bld_barbarian_civic_minor_chieftain_3_legendary"],"from":["att_bld_barbarian_civic_minor_chieftain_1_legendary"],"variants":[{"id":"att_bld_barbarian_civic_minor_chieftain_2_legendaryatt_cult_barbarian","subcultures":["att_sub_cult_barbarian_celtic","att_sub_cult_barbarian_germanic_east","att_sub_cult_barbarian_germanic_north","att_sub_cult_barbarian_germanic_west"],"faction":"","name":"Chieftain's Hall","picture":"att_bld_barbarian_civic_greathall_2_large","short":"Power is a curious thing. Invisible, yet must be seen to be believed.","flavour":"Much of what we know of barbarian tribes comes from accounts set down by Roman and Greek writers, and they are often extremely critical. However, Gaius Cornelius Tacitus, in the book ‘Germania’, writes about the Germanic tribes with great respect. He describes a people who were very open and hospitable, and who loved nothing more than feasting and celebrating great deeds. He was also impressed that they resolved all tribal issues at a council where every man's voice could be heard. Also remarking on the fidelity shown within Germanic marriages, there was one aspect of their lives that Tacitus found deplorable and barbaric - he thoroughly disapproved of “their habitual drunkenness”."}],"effects":[{"text":"Food consumption: -5","tone":"bad"},{"text":"Wealth: 150 from culture","tone":"good"},{"text":"Food consumption: -20","tone":"bad"},{"text":"Public order: +7","tone":"good"},{"text":"Enables agent recruitment: Spies","tone":"good"}],"units":[],"garrison":["att_ger_gothic_falxmen","rex_celt_caled_band_raiders","rex_celt_pict_band_raiders","rex_celt_scot_band_raiders","rex_germ_band_raiders","rex_germ_band_raiders_franc","rex_germ_band_raiders_nord","rex_germ_band_raiders_sax","rex_germ_band_raiders_sueb","rex_goth_band_gaizjane_vandals","rex_sarm_nobles_warband_g","rex_slav_ant_band_warspear","rex_slav_ven_band_warspear"]},{"key":"att_bld_barbarian_civic_minor_chieftain_3_legendary","tier":3,"cost":1500,"turns":5,"upkeep":0,"capital_only":false,"religion":"","resource":"","techs":[{"key":"att_barbarian_civil_supplant_government_officials","name":"Sacred King"}],"upgrades":["att_bld_barbarian_civic_minor_chieftain_4_legendary"],"from":["att_bld_barbarian_civic_minor_chieftain_2_legendary"],"variants":[{"id":"att_bld_barbarian_civic_minor_chieftain_3_legendaryatt_cult_barbarian","subcultures":["att_sub_cult_barbarian_celtic","att_sub_cult_barbarian_germanic_east","att_sub_cult_barbarian_germanic_north","att_sub_cult_barbarian_germanic_west"],"faction":"","name":"Chieftain's Hold","picture":"att_bld_barbarian_civic_chieftain_3_large","short":"It is important for men to gather and share the joys of life.","flavour":"As barbarian society developed the need for larger settlements was greater, in order to house their ever-larger populations. This gave rise to an increase in the number of fortified settlements built on areas with natural defences such as hilltops, cliffs or river banks. Stone or wooden defences were then added, setting them apart from smaller settlements. Some historians argue that the addition of fortifications was not a defensive decision but was, in fact, a matter of tribal prestige. As such, a tribe could have more than one fortified settlement and their sizes varied significantly. Julius Caesar, in his study of the Gauls, named their versions 'oppidum', noting that the differing sizes appeared to indicate the power structure within each tribe."}],"effects":[{"text":"Food consumption: -10","tone":"bad"},{"text":"Enables agent recruitment: Champions","tone":"good"},{"text":"Wealth: 250 from culture","tone":"good"},{"text":"Food consumption: -50","tone":"bad"},{"text":"Public order: +10","tone":"good"},{"text":"Enables agent recruitment: Spies","tone":"good"}],"units":["rex_germ_gisind","rex_goth_comites"],"garrison":["att_ger_gothic_falxmen","rex_celt_caled_band_raiders","rex_celt_pict_band_raiders","rex_celt_scot_band_raiders","rex_germ_band_raiders","rex_germ_band_raiders_franc","rex_germ_band_raiders_nord","rex_germ_band_raiders_sax","rex_germ_band_raiders_sueb","rex_goth_band_gaizjane_vandals","rex_sarm_nobles_warband_g","rex_slav_ant_band_warspear","rex_slav_ven_band_warspear"]},{"key":"att_bld_barbarian_civic_minor_chieftain_4_legendary","tier":4,"cost":2000,"turns":6,"upkeep":0,"capital_only":false,"religion":"","resource":"Timber","techs":[{"key":"att_barbarian_civil_influenced_legislation","name":"Royal Council"}],"upgrades":["att_bld_barbarian_civic_minor_chieftain_5_legendary"],"from":["att_bld_barbarian_civic_minor_chieftain_3_legendary"],"variants":[{"id":"att_bld_barbarian_civic_minor_chieftain_4_legendaryatt_cult_barbarian","subcultures":["att_sub_cult_barbarian_celtic","att_sub_cult_barbarian_germanic_east","att_sub_cult_barbarian_germanic_north","att_sub_cult_barbarian_germanic_west"],"faction":"","name":"Chieftain's Keep","picture":"att_bld_barbarian_civic_chieftain_4_large","short":"A wise warlord keeps his strongest men about him at all times.","flavour":"As barbarian society developed the need for larger settlements was greater, in order to house their ever-larger populations. This gave rise to an increase in the number of fortified settlements built on areas with natural defences such as hilltops, cliffs or river banks. Stone or wooden defences were then added, setting them apart from smaller settlements. Some historians argue that the addition of fortifications was not a defensive decision but was, in fact, a matter of tribal prestige. As such, a tribe could have more than one fortified settlement and their sizes varied significantly. Julius Caesar, in his study of the Gauls, named their versions 'oppidum', noting that the differing sizes appeared to indicate the power structure within each tribe."}],"effects":[{"text":"Replenishment: +1%","tone":"good"},{"text":"Food consumption: -20","tone":"bad"},{"text":"Enables agent recruitment: Champions","tone":"good"},{"text":"Wealth: 410 from culture","tone":"good"},{"te